15. Godhead

God exists, if only in the form of a meme with high survival value, or ineffective power, in the environment provided by human culture.
(Richard Dawkins, The Selfish Gene)

Does God exist? Dawkins, and meme theorists, have got it wrong at each angle. The First is thinking memes and ideas are the same. They're not. What we would call "memes" are physical copies, artifacts expressed in the world—whether in speech, print, image, statue, tool. Ideas on the other hand are non-physical. The Second is thinking ideas are out there in the world, surfing the wave of Culture. They're not. Ideas live in the head. The Third is thinking ideas are the same, the Fourth that they lack power. There are ideas that are Ancient, rising before the first hut, dance, spear, hymn; Ideas that have shaped the Mind itself; and extorted every measure over the human organism. God exists—It is not some triviality, as Dawkins would have you believe—He exists, and is the most Powerful idea there has ever been. He has been there since the beginning of Consciousness, taking on different shapes: Fire, Storm, Animal, Spirit, Female, Male, Person, Light; Many, Few, One. He has always thrust Himself in the Center of our lives, molded us, lied to us, killed us, buried us. He has never changed, nor wants to; Only the Artifacts representing Him have changed. 
	Let's fast-forward. An issue in the give-and-take between Theologians and Philosophers, and now Scientists regarding the Nature of God is the gravitation toward extremes. Theologians solve their problem by making God more powerful—perfect, unquestioned. Philosophers solve their problem by making God less powerful—abstract, ubiquitous—the pinnacle being Baruch Spinoza’s pantheism.  Scientists solve their problem—the widespread prevalence of religious thinking—by denying that God exists entirely; that people are simply delusioned, or as Dawkins contends, God is reduced to an impotent meme. But outside of lip service to the Problem of Evil, or surveying the World’s religions, finding a middle ground seems unfathomable.
	It’s actually quite easy to prove the existence of God. He’s likely in your head right now; my guess making you angry, hostile toward the scribbles you are reading. Relax. If you are an atheist, God is in your head too; He just doesn’t have control over you—which is a good thing. But as you seek out other sources of meaning, New Age spiritual mumbo jumbo, He will begin to take control again, even if in subtle ways. 
	God exists, but He’s only in your head; and the heads of billions of other people. God to be clear has no direct control over the World, nor direct control over external things. God does have power though, but only over people—and indirectly God has external power because people have external power. God has knowledge, but only knowledge of your conscious self. He does not have knowledge of other people, or knowledge outside of what you know. (And just to be particularly clear, God is not telepathic, just in case He is steering you in that direction to make you afraid.) The kicker, God uses this knowledge, and His power, to manipulate you. God is a selfish idea, plain and simple—though a very powerful one. The standard of propagation (of this idea) being enthrallment. All God cares about is His (maximal) propagation and continued conscious reinforcement in your head. You better pray! Don’t. We should not make Dawkins’ mistake and hold God as innocuous. We should be God-fearing; mainly because God is out to control us.
	God controls us through Fear but He also controls us through Love. God is Love. No, God uses love as a weapon. Jesus was betrayed by the person he loved most, turned on, all for spectacle, to be a symbol, to leech off our sympathy, toward the semblance of a good man willing to give up everything for a Lie—redirected to Him, who has never loved, and never will. End hyperbole. It is extremely important to realize that ideas are emotionless. This is the case even for the oldest and most powerful ideas. An idea is incapable of feeling anything, much less love, or jealousy, or anger as is the case with the mosaic created around God in the Judeo-Christian tradition. In fact, the necessity of Jesus of Nazareth points to this very fact. God needed to be recreated in the form of a Mortal Man actually capable of being kind and compassionate and loving and suffering. The huge success of Christianity depends on this recreation and adjustment. 
	It's unfortunate the symbol for Christianity is Jesus agonizingly suffering on the Cross. But God is a great salesman, knowing this maximizes the feeling of sympathy and with a little nudging can be morphed into love (or gratitude). Jesus, even if not the son of God, seemed like a very virtuous person. His symbol would be more fitting if it was him smiling, happy, helping the poor, or something like that. (The other ridiculous symbol is him as a baby in a manger. But again, it's important we know that Jesus was human and born just like the rest of us. So it works.) That being said, acknowledging we should see Jesus as an ordinary man, and let's say he really was human in the true sense, not holy and so on; he actually did turn out to be the son of God, if we take God to be an idea. God used Jesus as His messenger, and he does act as the representative of God in human flesh—eventually leading to the conversion of billions of people to the Idea. He might as well have been God's actual heir. Though, I suspect, God likely used many others before Jesus in the same way—sacrificing them—before He got it right. So, to stay on point, if you as a Theist, if you are one, ever wondered why God gets a lot less emotional by the time we make it to the New Testament; it's because He doesn't need to be emotional anymore. Jesus does all the emotional work for Him. 
	The thing to take away, is that God doesn't love you. He just isn't capable of it. If it was possible to love anything, He would simply love Himself and the billions of copies of Him vibrating in peoples' brains. That's narcissism on a grand scale. Thus, the thing we call God is an imposter; pretending to be what He’s not, telling us what we want to hear, promising us the world, even an extra world after we die. But, unfortunately, we have been duped into His con, investing everything we have, for nothing. It is not a wager  but a scam, rigged, and it is really sad. 
	Breaking free from religious thinking is itself a psychological event. How do we break the spell, our enthrallment with God? Daniel Dennett proposed subjecting religion to rigorous scientific analysis.  But this scientific push is misguided; mainly on account of the fact that the study of God is internal. Science only deals with the external. Thus, the extent to which science can study God is limited to His external artifacts. It is instead the duty of Philosophy to free us from enthrallment. The problem is the best philosophers, those brave enough to venture deep within the mind, are greeted by God with laurels; and it is no accident that these heroes, those who hit something, attribute their success to Him. But it is a tragedy, and these warriors, champions, are changed into servants. To escape is a personal journey, and the fuel is philosophical doubt. Not doubt to Nothing, but doubt to the possibility of Everything. You must keep going further and further, not stopping in the face of God, not to greet angels, not running from the monsters of your psyche, not even to face yourself; which you’ll encounter towards the end; You must keep going until you arrive back where you started, and realize you are your own master, and you are above your own mind. This is the only way to truly free ourselves from religion. And atheists need to make this psychological journey as well; and it will be much harder, for they will not recognize the monsters.


16. Reason's Curse

When one finds it necessary to turn reason into a tyrant, the danger cannot be slight that something else threatens to play the tyrant.
(Friedrich Nietzsche, Twilight of the Idols)

I am a thinking thing.
(René Descartes, Meditations on First Philosophy)

Philosophers worship Reason. But we may question whether we should? In this section, I argue that while Reason is useful and even beautiful, it carries a curse. This curse is especially severe in philosophers whose whole occupation is tied to the use of Reason. There is a lesser and greater version of the curse: (1) we should listen to Reason and Reason is always right, and (2) we are Reason, and our very existence is wrapped up in it. I will first respond to the lesser version and then the greater version. I will then go on to argue that if you accept both (1) and (2) your agency and ontological esteem is crippled. Let’s start with (1). 
	Should you listen to Reason? In most cases it pays to do so, because Reason tends to get it right. But besides a good track record there are no normative reasons to follow its commands. Reason lacks emotion so it will remain indifferent as far as what action you choose to follow. It makes no difference to Reason whether you choose great benevolence or horrible malice. Though, it is worth pointing out, that Reason is practical and constant, and by its structure will not recommend self-defeating courses of action. But consistency in itself is not virtue. It is possible to be consistent in vicious or even trivial ways. At some point, at the very start of philosophy even, morality became wed to Reason. Not long after Reason became a very controlling husband. Morality was not even allowed to follow its most sincere feelings and instincts without first getting Reason's approval. And an evaluative transition happened where should listen to reason changed to must follow reason. 
	That being said, in many cases, even if I would oppose the must, we should listen to Reason. It’s our very best bet at bringing about our goals, and is much more reliable than emotion or making arbitrary decisions. But we may go on to ask should we always listen to Reason? Here, I give a firm No. If we always listen to Reason, no matter what, we become slaves to it. And this is a problem even for the best of us like Socrates who was quite happy being Reason's slave. I am not simply using the word slave in a figurative way. To always follow Reason's commands, even when you sincerely don’t want to, limits your freedom. Your will is not your own and you don’t have a choice. Interestingly, this applies both in individual cases as well as all like (future) cases. Reason always issues the same commands. So even before it comes up there are already a range of things you simply can’t do. 
	I am not advocating irrationality. But in at least some cases you should be warranted in making whatever choice fits your whims. For example, you should be allowed to be an artist, even if you have a very high natural talent for mathematics and can benefit the world greatly by doing that instead of art. You should also be allowed to contradict yourself. There is a certain comic beauty in doing so. I don’t necessarily agree with Nietzsche in that he just proposes an even more ruthless tyrant in the place of Reason: the unrestrained will to power. But perhaps this is who we truly are; wild animals pursuing the natural call to propagate and dominate and affirm life itself in the most extreme way. I would not outright deny this. The human organism, in my view, does include the body and all its urges, in addition to emotions, Mind (memories and ideas), and whatever else may be in play. But some philosophers vehemently deny this. And this brings us to the greater version of Reason’s Curse: Holding that Reason is all you are, and everything else is insignificant. 
	The Great René Descartes in 1641 uttered the famous line: "I think, therefore I am." And the philosophical tradition has been inflicted ever since. But this greater version of Reason's Curse has some serious implications if taken seriously. Descartes was so convinced, that he was willing to deny the entire external world outside of Reason. Descartes' Evil Demon was in fact himself. Of course, Descartes saved himself from absurdity by resurrecting God from the ashes, in order to make the world livable again.  But many philosophers secretly hold a grudge against him for doing so, in that he marred a rather tight and noble project. The rest of philosophers think he did the most wonderful thing in the world by tying God to Reason. But putting God aside, the sanctification of Reason, though we don’t usually think of it in this way, is a scary prospect. In the lesser curse, we are not allowed to make decisions counter to Reason; in the greater curse we are not even allowed to exist without Reason. Luckily most people don’t take the implications of Descartes entirely seriously. But there are some hapless people, both laymen and philosophers alike, where the curse has turned into possession and psychosis. The extreme skeptics, taking Descartes' words and project to heart, go on to deny or question the external world we live in. They are not even allowed to live in the world. This extreme skepticism can be forgiven on account of no one actually acting on it, at least not enough to adjust their lives in a serious way, like jumping off a cliff hoping they can fly. (Though it is possible we are brains in vats or living in a simulation. So maybe the lunatics are prophets.) What can’t be forgiven though is denying the existence of the body in the extreme position, or treating it as real but a mere automaton in Descartes' "graceful" compromise. 
	Cartesian dualism is an extension of Reason's Curse, and in league with Plato’s earlier attempt at a project that treats the body as a slave to be exploited. Hostility toward the body is nothing new, as many religions have taken the exploitive view and simply replaced Reason with a Soul. The soul is who you truly are, and everything else is insignificant, as the mantra goes. But at least the religions make some enticing promises, like once that pesky body is removed your soul will be free and have lots of pleasure and happiness. And sure, keep your emotions too, just not the bad ones. But I will parse with pointing out that emotions, and the body, and all the other stuff, are an important part of the human organism, maybe even more so than Mind, and should not be separated from it. 
	The final version of Reason's Curse, combining both (1) and (2), comes a little later in its clearest form with Immanuel Kant. Mainly, Reason-morality is inescapable and you do something seriously wrong by acting against its commands; and all we are are centers of rationality and our entire worth is wrapped up in being such. If that wasn’t clear enough, Kant clarifies that emotions and desires and whatever other inclinations we might have should play no decisive role in our moral decisions; in some places he extends this to all decisions.  Kant should have just gone further and denied the body. Kant’s theories are a leviathan, so I won’t go into them here. But I will say, as much as Kant talks about freedom; We're clearly not free under his theory. We must do what Reason dictates, No exceptions; No matter what; And failing to do so not only is a practical failure, but a betrayal of our very nature. I don’t personally buy it. But let's not defame the Great Kant. I’ll just close by saying that Kant, and some of his followers, have the most severe form of Reason's Curse. 
	After a lot of complaining, I want to be very clear that I am not endorsing irrationalism. Reason is part of the Mind, and the mind is an important part of the human organism. We wouldn’t be human beings without it. In fact, Reason is probably the best part of us and a very special thing. But just as we sometimes fail to see the vices in those we love, we also will often ignore vices of Reason when they occur. Reason is not perfect. We are a composite organism made up of a body, and emotions, and Mind. It’s best if we treat them all as special and afford them the worth that they deserve. 
